<%# Which movement a set is of, asked as a search box rather than as a scroll wheel. #525.

    The control this replaces is one <select> carrying every movement the account can see:
    fifty-four library lifts plus their own, which is eighty-four on the reporting account
    and grows with every accessory they add. On a desktop that is a long menu. On a phone it
    is a wheel with eighty-four detents, spun with a thumb, mid-session, between sets, to
    find "Single-Arm DB Row" -- and the two optgroups help exactly as much as two signposts
    help on a road you are already driving past. Typing four letters is the whole feature.

    **The list stays in the page and the filtering happens in the browser. There is no
    search endpoint, and that is the argument rather than the shortcut.**

    The issue asks for this "with htmx", and htmx is the wrong mechanism here for the reason
    #516 has just finished paying for. A picker driven by hx-get per keystroke is a picker
    that needs the network to answer a question the page already knows the answer to, and
    this app is used in basements. The failure mode is not a slow list, it is htmx's swap
    rule: a request that does not arrive swaps nothing, so a lifter three sets into squats
    types "row", watches an empty box, and has no way to log the set they just did. That is
    the exact silence #516 shipped a banner to end, reintroduced on the one form that writes
    training by hand.

    The size of the thing settles it. The whole list is about three kilobytes of markup and
    is *already* in the page today -- the select has always carried every option -- so
    keeping it costs nothing new and removes the network from the interaction entirely. A
    server round trip per keystroke over gym LTE is two hundred milliseconds of nothing
    happening, which on a search box reads as broken even when it works. Filtering an array
    of eighty-four strings takes no measurable time at all.

    So the honest answer to "typeahead search with htmx" is that the search half is worth
    having and the htmx half is not, and that this page consequently loads no htmx at all --
    which is #237's rule (50KB is not paid for by a page that has no use for it) applied to
    the one screen where a phone is holding one bar of signal. If the movement list ever
    outgrows the page -- thousands of rows, or matching on notes the page does not carry --
    the endpoint becomes worth its cost, and the shape here is what it would hang off: the
    listbox is built from a list, and where that list comes from is one function.

    **The <select> is still the control.** It is not a leftover; it is where the answer
    lives. The script hides it and drives it, so:

      - With no JavaScript -- a blocked script, a failed asset, a browser in a state nobody
        predicted -- the page is byte-for-byte the form that shipped before this, wheel and
        all. Nothing about picking a movement is new enough to be worth losing entirely.
      - The edit form still opens on the movement the set is already on, because
        _exercise_options still marks it selected and the script reads the selection from
        the select rather than deciding one. The comment on that partial explains what is at
        stake: a menu with nothing marked submits its first option, so a picker that forgot
        its selection would quietly move an edited set to whichever movement sorts first.
      - A set posted without the picker being touched posts the same exercise_id it always
        did. There is no hidden input to keep in step, no change event to have missed, and
        no state that exists only in JavaScript -- the one thing the browser submits is the
        one thing the server rendered.

    A hidden input plus a text box would have been fewer moving parts and is the wrong trade:
    it makes the value a thing only the script can set, and the failure it buys is silent
    and is somebody's training log.

    The two groups survive into the results, and they are real information rather than
    decoration -- "is this the library Bent-Over Row or the one I made with my own name for
    it" is the question a lifter is asking when they go looking for their own variant. They
    are read off the optgroups, so the markup that draws them stays in one place, and a
    group with nothing matching in it disappears along with its heading.

    Within a group the order is the select's own, which is the order the route asks for --
    alphabetical since #551, where the argument for it lives (`Exercise.library_first_by_name`).
    Ranking matches by how well they match would read better on a flat list and cannot be done
    without dissolving the groups, and the groups are worth more: eight matches under two
    headings is a shorter question than eight matches in relevance order. What the ranking
    would have bought is mostly bought by the filter itself, since four letters is usually the
    difference between eighty-four rows and two.

    Matching is every whitespace-separated term appearing somewhere in the name, folded to
    lowercase. Substrings rather than prefixes, because the word a lifter reaches for is
    rarely the first one: "row" has to find Single-Arm DB Row, and "db row" has to find it
    without knowing that "Single-Arm" comes first. %>
<div data-exercise-picker>
  <%# The fallback, and the thing that submits. Hidden by the script below rather than by a
      class, because a container that is display:none still submits every control inside it
      -- only `disabled` stops that -- and because `hidden` takes the select out of the tab
      order and out of the accessibility tree in one attribute. Two labels rather than one
      shared between the two controls: a <label for> names exactly one element, and the one
      that is not on screen goes away with the branch it belongs to.

      It was unlabelled until now, which is worth naming as a bug rather than as a tidy-up:
      a select whose only description is the option currently showing tells a screen reader
      "Back Squat, combo box" and nothing about what the question is. %>
  <div data-picker-native>
    <label for="exercise_id" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Movement</label>
    <select id="exercise_id" name="exercise_id"
           class="<%= field_style %> mt-2 block w-full py-1.5 pr-10 text-gray-900 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6">
      <%== render('sets/_exercise_options') %>
    </select>
  </div>

  <%# The combobox, drawn only once the script that drives it has run. `hidden` here and
      revealed below, rather than the other way round, so that the working control is the one
      on screen in every state: a browser that never runs the script shows the select, and one
      that does swaps the pair before it paints, because the script is inline and immediately
      after the markup it needs.

      The ARIA is the APG combobox-with-list-autocomplete pattern written out: the input
      carries the combobox role, says whether the popup is open, names the listbox it
      controls, and points aria-activedescendant at the option under the keyboard cursor --
      which is how a listbox is navigated without focus ever leaving the text box. The
      options are not focusable and must not be; a Tab that walked eighty-four of them would
      be a worse wheel than the one this replaces.

      text-base and min-h-11 for the same two reasons they appear on every other control this
      app puts under a thumb: iOS Safari zooms the page in on a field whose text is under
      16px and the way back out is a pinch, and 44px is the size of a thumb. %>
  <div data-picker-search hidden class="relative">
    <label for="exercise-search" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Movement</label>
    <input type="text" id="exercise-search" data-picker-input
           role="combobox" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exercise-listbox"
           aria-autocomplete="list" aria-describedby="exercise-search-help"
           autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="none" spellcheck="false"
           enterkeyhint="done"
           class="<%= field_style %> mt-2 block min-h-11 w-full py-1.5 text-base text-gray-900 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6">
    <p id="exercise-search-help" class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">Type part of the name to narrow the list.</p>
    <%# Overlaid rather than in the flow, so that opening it does not push Weight and Reps
        down the screen every time the box takes focus. z-10 clears the form and nothing
        else: this is not the session screen, which stacks four fixed things at its foot. %>
    <div data-picker-popup hidden
         class="absolute z-10 mt-1 max-h-72 w-full overflow-y-auto rounded-md bg-white py-1 shadow-lg ring-1 ring-gray-300">
      <ul id="exercise-listbox" data-picker-list role="listbox" aria-label="Movements"></ul>
      <%# Outside the listbox, deliberately. A listbox may only contain options and groups,
          so a "nothing matches" row inside it would be a sentence announced as an option --
          and an option a lifter could try to choose. The live region below says it out loud;
          this is the same news for somebody who can see the box. %>
      <p data-picker-empty hidden class="px-3 py-2 text-sm text-gray-500">
        No movement matches that.
      </p>
    </div>
    <%# What just happened, said once, to the reader who cannot watch the list shorten --
        the same argument and the same shape as the session screen's announcement region
        (#336). Polite, so it waits for a gap rather than cutting across the letters being
        echoed back as they are typed, and empty on first render because a live region
        announces changes to itself and one that arrives carrying its sentence says nothing. %>
    <p data-picker-announce role="status" aria-live="polite" class="sr-only"></p>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  (function () {
    var picker = document.querySelector('[data-exercise-picker]');
    if (!picker) { return; }

    var select = picker.querySelector('select');
    var native = picker.querySelector('[data-picker-native]');
    var search = picker.querySelector('[data-picker-search]');
    var input = picker.querySelector('[data-picker-input]');
    var popup = picker.querySelector('[data-picker-popup]');
    var list = picker.querySelector('[data-picker-list]');
    var empty = picker.querySelector('[data-picker-empty]');
    var announce = picker.querySelector('[data-picker-announce]');

    // An account with nothing to pick from keeps the select. There is no list to search and
    // a search box over nothing is a worse answer than an empty menu, which at least looks
    // like what it is.
    if (!select || !select.options.length) { return; }

    // The class lists, written here rather than toggled as modifiers, because the active
    // row changes its text colour and Tailwind gives no guarantee about which of two colour
    // utilities on one element wins. Assigning the whole list is the only way to be sure
    // that text-white beats text-gray-700 rather than depending on the order the stylesheet
    // happened to compile in.
    var ROW = 'flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center px-3 text-base';
    var IDLE = ' text-gray-700';
    var ACTIVE = ' bg-sky-800 text-white';
    // The movement the set is on, called out among the rest. The input already shows its
    // name; this is for the moment the list is open over it and the name is no longer the
    // thing being read.
    var CHOSEN = ' font-semibold';

    // Every option, flattened out of the select with the group it came from carried along.
    // Read once: the select does not change under this page, and rebuilding the list on
    // every keystroke would be work done for nothing.
    var movements = [];
    var groups = [];

    function collect() {
      var containers = select.querySelectorAll('optgroup');
      if (!containers.length) { containers = [select]; }
      for (var i = 0; i < containers.length; i++) {
        var label = containers[i].label || '';
        var options = containers[i].querySelectorAll('option');
        var members = [];
        for (var j = 0; j < options.length; j++) {
          members.push({ id: options[j].value, name: options[j].text, group: label });
        }
        if (members.length) { groups.push({ label: label, members: members }); }
        movements = movements.concat(members);
      }
    }

    // The listbox, built once from the groups above.
    //
    // Nothing here is assembled as a string of markup. Exercise names are typed by people
    // and this app has a spec file about what happens when one of them contains a less-than
    // sign; textContent cannot be talked into being an element, and innerHTML can.
    //
    // The nesting is what the APG's grouped listbox asks for: a presentational row holding a
    // visible heading and a role="group" of options that names it. A bare heading among the
    // options would be announced as an option, which is how "Library" becomes a movement
    // somebody can pick.
    function build() {
      for (var i = 0; i < groups.length; i++) {
        var wrapper = document.createElement('li');
        wrapper.setAttribute('role', 'presentation');
        var heading = document.createElement('span');
        heading.id = 'exercise-group-' + i;
        // Not uppercased by CSS. A browser draws an optgroup label in its own plain words and
        // so does this, which keeps what is on screen and what a screen reader says the same
        // string -- text-transform changes only the first of those.
        heading.className = 'block px-3 pb-1 pt-2 text-xs font-semibold text-gray-500';
        heading.textContent = groups[i].label;
        var inner = document.createElement('ul');
        inner.setAttribute('role', 'group');
        inner.setAttribute('aria-labelledby', heading.id);
        for (var j = 0; j < groups[i].members.length; j++) {
          var movement = groups[i].members[j];
          var row = document.createElement('li');
          row.setAttribute('role', 'option');
          row.setAttribute('aria-selected', 'false');
          row.id = 'exercise-option-' + movement.id;
          row.setAttribute('data-exercise-id', movement.id);
          row.textContent = movement.name;
          movement.element = row;
          inner.appendChild(row);
        }
        wrapper.appendChild(heading);
        wrapper.appendChild(inner);
        groups[i].element = wrapper;
        list.appendChild(wrapper);
      }
    }

    function chosenId() { return select.value; }

    function nameOf(id) {
      for (var i = 0; i < movements.length; i++) {
        if (movements[i].id === id) { return movements[i].name; }
      }
      return '';
    }

    // Which movements a query leaves standing. Every term has to appear somewhere in the
    // name, so the terms may be given in any order and none of them has to start a word:
    // "db row" finds Single-Arm DB Row, which is the search this feature exists for.
    function matches(movement, terms) {
      var name = movement.name.toLowerCase();
      for (var i = 0; i < terms.length; i++) {
        if (name.indexOf(terms[i]) === -1) { return false; }
      }
      return true;
    }

    // The whole list redrawn from the query, rather than the rows that changed patched.
    // There are eighty-odd of them and four things that can move any of them; deciding all
    // of it from the two pieces of state is shorter than the transitions would be, and it
    // cannot leave a heading standing over a group with nothing under it.
    var visible = [];
    var active = null;

    function draw(query) {
      var terms = query.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/).filter(function (term) { return term.length; });
      visible = [];
      for (var i = 0; i < groups.length; i++) {
        var shown = 0;
        for (var j = 0; j < groups[i].members.length; j++) {
          var movement = groups[i].members[j];
          var hit = matches(movement, terms);
          movement.element.hidden = !hit;
          if (hit) {
            shown++;
            visible.push(movement);
          }
        }
        groups[i].element.hidden = shown === 0;
      }
      empty.hidden = visible.length > 0;
      if (visible.indexOf(active) === -1) { activate(visible.length ? visible[0] : null); }
      paint();
    }

    // The keyboard cursor. It is aria-activedescendant rather than focus, which is the whole
    // of why the text box keeps the caret while the list is being walked -- and why the
    // options carry no tabindex: a Tab stop per movement would be the scroll wheel again
    // with worse manners.
    function activate(movement) {
      active = movement;
      if (movement) {
        input.setAttribute('aria-activedescendant', movement.element.id);
        movement.element.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' });
      } else {
        input.removeAttribute('aria-activedescendant');
      }
    }

    function paint() {
      var chosen = chosenId();
      for (var i = 0; i < movements.length; i++) {
        var movement = movements[i];
        var selected = movement.id === chosen;
        movement.element.setAttribute('aria-selected', selected ? 'true' : 'false');
        movement.element.className = ROW + (movement === active ? ACTIVE : IDLE) + (selected ? CHOSEN : '');
      }
    }

    function said(sentence) { announce.textContent = sentence; }

    function open(query) {
      draw(query);
      popup.hidden = false;
      input.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'true');
    }

    // Closing always puts the text back to the movement that is actually going to be posted.
    // That invariant is the point of the whole control: the box is a search field and the
    // select is the answer, and a box left reading "sing" over a set that is still on Back
    // Squat would be the form lying about what Save is about to do.
    function close() {
      popup.hidden = true;
      input.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
      input.removeAttribute('aria-activedescendant');
      active = null;
      input.value = nameOf(chosenId());
      paint();
    }

    function choose(movement) {
      if (!movement) { return; }
      select.value = movement.id;
      close();
      // The group as well as the name, because "Bent-Over Row" from the library and
      // "Bent-Over Row" as somebody has redefined it for themselves are the same six
      // syllables and a different movement.
      said(movement.name + ', ' + movement.group);
    }

    function step(by) {
      if (!visible.length) { return; }
      var at = visible.indexOf(active);
      var next = at === -1 ? 0 : at + by;
      if (next < 0) { next = visible.length - 1; }
      if (next >= visible.length) { next = 0; }
      activate(visible[next]);
      paint();
    }

    collect();
    build();
    input.value = nameOf(chosenId());
    // The swap. Both halves in one place so there is no state in which neither control is
    // on screen, and after the list is built so the first tap has something to open.
    native.hidden = true;
    search.hidden = false;

    // Opening on focus rather than waiting for a keystroke, because the first thing a thumb
    // does to this box is tap it, and a tap that produces nothing reads as a dead control.
    // The text is selected so that typing replaces the movement already named rather than
    // appending to it -- on the edit form the box arrives full, and clearing it by hand with
    // a backspace key on a phone keyboard is exactly the friction this change is about.
    input.addEventListener('focus', function () {
      input.select();
      open('');
      activate(visible.filter(function (movement) { return movement.id === chosenId(); })[0] || visible[0]);
      paint();
    });

    input.addEventListener('input', function () {
      open(input.value);
      said(visible.length === 1 ? '1 movement' :
        (visible.length ? visible.length + ' movements' : 'No movement matches that.'));
    });

    input.addEventListener('keydown', function (event) {
      var key = event.key;
      if (key === 'ArrowDown' || key === 'ArrowUp') {
        // Down on a closed box opens it rather than moving anything, which is the one
        // keyboard gesture a native select has that a text box does not come with.
        if (popup.hidden) { open(input.value); } else { step(key === 'ArrowDown' ? 1 : -1); }
        event.preventDefault();
      } else if (key === 'Home' && !popup.hidden) {
        activate(visible[0]);
        paint();
        event.preventDefault();
      } else if (key === 'End' && !popup.hidden) {
        activate(visible[visible.length - 1]);
        paint();
        event.preventDefault();
      } else if (key === 'Enter') {
        // Only while the list is open, and only then. Enter on a closed box is a lifter
        // submitting the form from the keyboard, which is a thing this form has always let
        // them do and is not this control's to take away.
        //
        // With nothing matching there is nothing to commit, so Enter closes instead of doing
        // nothing at all. It is the same key on a phone -- enterkeyhint puts "Done" on it --
        // and a Done that leaves the list standing over the rest of the form is a control
        // somebody is stuck inside.
        if (!popup.hidden) {
          if (active) { choose(active); } else { close(); }
          event.preventDefault();
        }
      } else if (key === 'Escape') {
        // Back to the movement on the set, which is what close() means by closing. A lifter
        // who opened the picker by accident mid-edit gets out of it without having changed
        // anything, which on the edit form is the difference between a typo and a set filed
        // under the wrong lift.
        if (!popup.hidden) {
          close();
          event.preventDefault();
        }
      }
    });

    // mousedown rather than click, and refused rather than handled: letting the press move
    // focus would blur the input, and blur closes the popup and puts the text back, so the
    // click would land on a row that is no longer there. Preventing the default keeps the
    // caret in the box and the click still arrives. A tap on a phone synthesises both.
    popup.addEventListener('mousedown', function (event) { event.preventDefault(); });

    popup.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
      var row = event.target.closest('[data-exercise-id]');
      if (!row) { return; }
      choose(movements.filter(function (movement) { return movement.element === row; })[0]);
    });

    // Leaving the box is not a choice. Whatever half-typed word is in it goes, and the name
    // of the movement that will actually be posted comes back -- the same invariant close()
    // exists for, applied to the one way out of the control that is not a key press.
    input.addEventListener('blur', close);

    paint();
  })();
</script>
